1
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:12,540
It's inevitable. Accidents will happen in the laboratory. Accidents, by definition,

2
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:17,460
are unforeseen. But your knowledge of lab safety will help minimize the likelihood of

3
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:22,960
accidents. And in this lesson, we'll talk about safety equipment found in the lab. These

4
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:28,520
items may help you save a life if and when an accident occurs. When working in a lab

5
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:33,860
for the first time, look around and identify the location of the safety equipment. Every

6
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:38,880
lab must contain a safety shower and eye wash station. Both should be tested

7
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:43,780
weekly to ensure they're working properly and water is clean. If a chemical or flame

8
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:48,140
exposure has occurred, yell for help and immediately move to the nearest safety

9
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:52,620
shower. Remove the saturated clothing and thoroughly drench the affected skin

10
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:56,820
under the shower. If your clothes or skin are exposed to flame, then drench

11
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:02,280
your entire body and have someone call 911. The eye wash station is used for

12
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:07,020
rinsing your eyes if they're exposed to hazardous chemicals. Hold your eyes open

13
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:10,820
and thoroughly rinse for at least 10 minutes. The next piece of safety

14
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:15,900
equipment is the fire extinguisher. There are four types of fires. Class A

15
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:22,020
fires consist of ordinary combustibles like wood, cloth, and paper. These fires

16
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:27,260
can be extinguished by water or general purpose extinguishers. Class B fires

17
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:33,100
include organic solvents and flammable liquids and class C fires involve

18
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:38,800
electrical equipment. Class B and C fires must be smothered with chemical foam

19
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:43,820
extinguishers. Putting water on these fires will only make matters worse. Water

20
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:47,980
will actually cause the fire to spread and you can even electrocute yourself.

21
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:53,420
Class D fires involve combustible metals which aren't very common in the lab.

22
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:58,660
That's why most labs contain dry chemical fire extinguishers which are

23
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:03,020
installed close to the exits. Dry chemical extinguishers are effective

24
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:09,460
against class A, B, and C fires. If a fire occurs and it's too large for you to

25
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:14,700
extinguish, evacuate all personnel immediately and call 911. Don't attempt

26
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:18,700
to use a fire extinguisher unless you have been trained to do so by certified

27
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:23,260
trainers such as the local fire department. A fire blanket can be used to

28
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:31,740
extinguish small flames on work benches and floors. It can also be used to help

29
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:37,420
someone whose clothing is on fire. Never wrap someone while they're standing. This

30
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:41,820
can force the flames upward toward the head and neck area. Instead, help the

31
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:46,580
person to the floor, wrap them in the blanket, and help them roll until the

32
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:52,180
fire is out. Each lab should have a first aid kit that contains bandages and

33
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:57,100
antiseptic for minor injuries. Evacuation routes should be posted near the exits.

34
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:01,140
It's important that you know multiple evacuation routes in case one is

35
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:06,420
blocked. Another helpful piece of safety equipment is the chemical fume hood. It's

36
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:11,740
a ventilated enclosed work area that protects you from toxic vapors. Turn on

37
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:16,180
the exhaust fan and make sure the hood is venting properly. The opening is

38
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:20,820
covered by a window called a sash which can be raised and lowered. For most

39
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:26,540
applications, the sash should be open to either 8 or 16 inches. Never store

40
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:30,620
chemicals under the hood and always clean and remove materials when you're

41
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:35,380
finished working. Test your safety equipment regularly to make sure each

42
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:39,820
item is ready in case there's an emergency. In our next lesson, we'll

43
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:44,300
examine how your behavior in the lab can help keep you and your co-workers

44
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:46,580
safe.
